    // ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
    //  ۱: Jane Eyre
    // ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
    private fun story1() = EnStory(
        storyId = "adv_jane_eyre",
        chapters = listOf(
            EnChapter(1, "The Red Room", listOf(
                "My name is Jane Eyre, and I am an orphan, a small and plain child with no fortune, no beauty, and no friends in the world, save perhaps a few servants who pitied me.",
                "I live at Gateshead Hall, the home of my aunt, Mrs. Reed, a woman who took me in after my parents died but who has never shown me a single moment of genuine affection.",
                "My uncle, her husband, loved me as if I were his own child, and on his deathbed he made her promise to raise me as one of her own, but she has kept that promise only in the coldest and most literal sense.",
                "Her children, Eliza, Georgiana, and John, treat me with open contempt, and John in particular takes great pleasure in tormenting me, for he is a boy of cruel temper and idle habits.",
                "One afternoon, when I was quietly reading a book in a window seat, John approached me with his usual sneer and demanded that I come and stand before him.",
                "When I refused, he struck me hard across the face, and I fell against the door, bleeding from a cut on my head.",
                "Something inside me broke, and for the first time in my life, I fought back, striking him with all the strength that my small body could muster.",
                "My aunt rushed into the room, and seeing her precious son on the floor, she ordered the servants to drag me away and lock me in the red room.",
                "The red room was the chamber where my uncle had died, a large and solemn place with crimson curtains and heavy mahogany furniture, and it had not been entered since his death.",
                "I begged her not to lock me in there, for the room terrified me, but she was deaf to my pleas, and the servants carried me there and shut the door behind them.",
                "Alone in that dreadful chamber, surrounded by the memory of death, I began to imagine that I saw my uncle's ghost rising from the shadows to avenge me.",
                "I screamed and cried and pounded on the door, begging to be released, but no one came, and the silence of the house seemed to press against me like a weight.",
                "The hours passed in agony, and my terror grew until it consumed me entirely, until my mind could no longer distinguish between reality and nightmare.",
                "I fainted, and when I woke, I was lying in my own bed, and a kind servant named Bessie was sitting beside me, gently wiping my forehead with a cool cloth.",
                "Bessie was one of the few people at Gateshead who showed me any kindness, and she often sang to me and told me stories of the wider world beyond the walls of the house.",
                "She told me that I had been ill for some time, and that the doctor had come and gone, and that everyone in the house had been frightened by my screams.",
                "But when I asked if my aunt had come to see me, Bessie looked away, and I knew that she had not, and that she never would.",
                "Slowly, I recovered my strength, but I did not recover my peace of mind, for the terror of the red room had left a wound that would not heal.",
                "I spent many long days alone in my room, with nothing to do but read whatever books I could find and think about the life that I wished I had.",
                "My aunt told the servants that I was a liar and a troublemaker, and most of them believed her, and so I was treated with suspicion and coldness wherever I went.",
                "Only Bessie remained my friend, and even she could not protect me from the loneliness that pressed upon my heart like a heavy stone.",
                "I dreamed of a different life, a life far away from Gateshead Hall, a life where I would be loved and respected and treated as a human being.",
                "I did not know how such a life could ever come to be, for I was poor and plain and had no family to help me, but still I dreamed.",
                "One day, a tall and stern man came to the house, dressed in black and carrying a heavy cane, and he spoke with my aunt in the drawing room for a long time.",
                "His name was Mr. Brocklehurst, and he was the headmaster of a charity school called Lowood, where poor girls were sent to be educated and prepared for a life of humble service.",
                "My aunt told him that I was a child of deceitful and ungrateful character, and she advised him to keep a strict watch over my behavior at all times.",
                "I listened to her lies with a heart full of anger, but I did not dare to speak, for I had learned that speaking only brought more punishment.",
                "Mr. Brocklehurst looked at me with cold and suspicious eyes, and he asked me whether I liked reading the Bible and whether I intended to be a good child from now on.",
                "I answered him as politely as I could, but I could see that he had already decided what kind of girl I was, and nothing I said would change his mind.",
                "When he left, my aunt told me that I would be sent to Lowood School as soon as arrangements could be made, and I felt a strange mixture of fear and hope.",
                "Fear, because I did not know what awaited me in that unfamiliar place, and hope, because anything, even Lowood, seemed better than another day at Gateshead.",
                "The days that followed were filled with preparations for my departure, and I watched as Bessie packed my few belongings into a small trunk.",
                "She gave me a warm hug before I left, and she whispered to me that I was a good girl, and that I should not believe the cruel things that others said about me.",
                "I clung to those words as the carriage carried me away from the only home I had ever known, and I promised myself that I would prove them true.",
                "The journey was long and cold, and the road wound through hills and forests that I had never seen before, and by the time we arrived, it was dark and raining.",
                "A servant led me through the gates of Lowood and into a large, gloomy building where many girls were already gathered in a long, bare hall.",
                "I was given a plain uniform and a hard bed, and I was told that from this day forward, I would follow the rules of the school without question or complaint.",
                "As I lay in my bed that first night, listening to the wind howl outside the windows, I wondered what kind of life awaited me in this strange and unforgiving place.",
                "And so began my years at Lowood, years that would shape me more than any other period of my life, years that would teach me both the cruelty and the kindness of the world."
            )),
            EnChapter(2, "Lowood School", listOf(
                "The next morning, I was woken before dawn by the sound of a bell, and I rose with the other girls to dress and go down to the cold and crowded schoolroom.",
                "The food was thin and tasteless, and there was never enough of it, and the water in the pitchers was often frozen in the winter months.",
                "The teachers were strict and often unkind, and they seemed to take a certain pleasure in punishing the girls for the smallest infractions of the rules.",
                "Mr. Brocklehurst himself came to the school only occasionally, but his visits were always dreaded, for he was a man of harsh and hypocritical character.",
                "On one of his visits, he called me to the front of the room, and in front of all the girls, he accused me of being a liar and told them to shun me.",
                "I was humiliated beyond words, and I stood there in silence, unable to defend myself, unable to explain that my aunt had lied about me.",
                "But there was one girl, a quiet and gentle girl named Helen Burns, who looked at me with compassion instead of contempt, and who sought me out after the ordeal was over.",
                "Helen was older than me, and she was often punished for her carelessness and her lack of order, but she bore her punishments with a patience that I could not understand.",
                "She told me that forgiveness was a greater virtue than revenge, and that we should love our enemies and pray for those who persecute us, no matter how cruel they might be.",
                "I did not fully understand her words at the time, but I came to see that there was a deep wisdom in her gentle spirit, a wisdom that I would carry with me for the rest of my life.",
                "Helen and I became close friends, and we spent many hours together, reading and talking and sharing our thoughts about the world and our place in it.",
                "She was the first person I had ever met who loved me without condition, who saw me as I truly was, and who believed in me even when I did not believe in myself.",
                "One winter, a terrible fever swept through the school, and many girls fell ill, and the classrooms were filled with the sounds of coughing and weeping.",
                "The food was even worse than usual, and the cold was unbearable, and the doctors could do nothing to stop the spread of the disease.",
                "Helen became very ill, and I was told that I could not see her, for the sick girls were kept in a separate part of the building.",
                "But one night, when the house was quiet and the matron was asleep, I crept out of my bed and made my way to Helen's room, determined to see her one last time.",
                "She was lying in her bed, pale and thin and barely able to speak, but when she saw me, her face lit up with a smile of pure and gentle joy.",
                "She told me that she was not afraid of death, and that she looked forward to the peace that awaited her, and she asked me to hold her hand.",
                "I held her hand all through the night, and I listened to her breathe, and I prayed that she might live, but I knew in my heart that she would not.",
                "As the dawn broke, she whispered to me that she was ready, and she closed her eyes, and she slipped away from this world without a single sound.",
                "I was overcome with grief, and I cried until I had no tears left, for I had lost the only friend I had ever truly known.",
                "After Helen's death, the school was investigated, and conditions were improved, and the food became more plentiful and the teachers more humane.",
                "But for me, the school had lost its light, and the years that followed were marked by a strange and aching emptiness that never quite went away.",
                "I continued my studies with diligence and determination, and I became one of the best students in the school, admired by my teachers and respected by my classmates.",
                "When I had completed my own education, I was offered a position as a teacher, and I accepted it gladly, for I had no other place to go and no other family to return to.",
                "For two years, I taught at Lowood, and I did my work well, but I could not shake the feeling that my life was passing me by, that there was more to existence than the walls of this school.",
                "I wanted to see the world, to meet new people, to experience the freedom that I had been denied since the day I was born.",
                "I wanted to find a place where I could be myself, where I could love and be loved, where I could live a life of meaning and purpose and joy.",
                "So I placed an advertisement in a newspaper, offering my services as a governess to any family that might need one, and I waited to see what fate would send my way.",
                "Weeks passed with no response, and I began to fear that my advertisement had been a foolish mistake, that I would be trapped at Lowood forever.",
                "But then, one day, a letter arrived, and it was from a place called Thornfield Hall, and it offered me a position teaching a young French girl named Adele.",
                "I accepted the offer immediately, and I prepared to leave Lowood, and as the carriage carried me toward my new home, I felt a thrill of hope that I had not felt in years.",
                "I did not know what awaited me at Thornfield, but I was ready to face whatever it might be, for I had learned by now that life was a series of challenges that must be met with courage.",
                "The journey was long, and the countryside was wild and beautiful, and by the time I saw the tall gray walls of Thornfield rising before me, I felt as though I was entering a new world entirely.",
                "I was greeted by a kind and elderly housekeeper named Mrs. Fairfax, who showed me to my room and told me that the master of the house was away on business.",
                "I met my young pupil, Adele, and I found her to be a sweet and lively child, eager to learn and full of energy and curiosity.",
                "As the days passed, I began to feel at home in the old house, and I grew to love its quiet corners and its mysterious history and the strange silence that seemed to fill its halls.",
                "But there was something unsettling about Thornfield, something that I could not quite name, something that would make itself known to me soon enough.",
                "For the old house held a secret, a dark and terrible secret, and that secret would change the course of my life in ways that I could never have imagined.",
                "And so the first chapter of my new life began, in a strange and beautiful house, in a world that was both welcoming and mysterious, and I knew that nothing would ever be the same."
            )),
            EnChapter(3, "Thornfield Hall", listOf(
                "The winter months at Thornfield passed quietly, and I settled into a routine of teaching Adele in the mornings and walking in the grounds in the afternoons.",
                "Mrs. Fairfax was kind to me, and the other servants were polite, and yet I often felt a strange loneliness, as though I were waiting for something that had not yet arrived.",
                "Sometimes, late at night, I would hear strange sounds in the corridors, a laughter that seemed to come from somewhere deep in the house, a laughter that was not quite human.",
                "I asked Mrs. Fairfax about these sounds, and she told me that they came from one of the servants, a woman named Grace Poole, who was prone to strange moods and uncontrollable fits.",
                "I was not satisfied with this explanation, but I did not press her further, for I had learned by now that the rich kept secrets, and that they did not like to be questioned about them.",
                "One cold afternoon in January, I decided to walk to the village of Millcote to post a letter, and as I walked along the frozen road, I saw a rider approaching on horseback.",
                "The horse slipped on the ice, and the rider fell, and I rushed to help him to his feet, and when I saw his face, I was struck by its dark and brooding beauty.",
                "He was a man of middle age, with a strong and rugged face and eyes that seemed to hold the memory of many sorrows, and I felt a strange sense of recognition, as though I had known him all my life.",
                "He spoke to me with a gruff and imperious manner, and he asked me who I was and what I was doing on the road so far from the house.",
                "I told him that I was the governess at Thornfield Hall, and he looked at me with a curious expression, and then he mounted his horse and rode away without another word.",
                "When I returned to Thornfield, I learned that the rider had been Mr. Rochester himself, the master of the house, and that he had returned from his travels abroad.",
                "That evening, I was summoned to the drawing room, and I found myself once again in the presence of the man I had helped on the road, and we spoke for many hours.",
                "He was unlike any man I had ever met, for he spoke to me not as a servant but as an equal, and he seemed to find my directness and my honesty refreshing.",
                "He asked me many questions about my life, and I answered him truthfully, telling him about my childhood at Gateshead and my years at Lowood and my hopes for the future.",
                "In return, he told me something of his own life, and though his stories were strange and often troubling, I found myself drawn to him more and more with each passing day.",
                "There was a darkness in Mr. Rochester, a sadness that he carried with him wherever he went, and I sensed that it was the source of much of his strange behavior.",
                "But there was also a warmth in him, a kindness that he showed to me in a thousand small ways, and I began to feel for him something that I had never felt for anyone before.",
                "One night, as I lay in my bed, I heard a strange noise coming from Mr. Rochester's room, a noise that sounded like the crackling of flames.",
                "I rushed into his room without thinking, and I found his bed curtains on fire, and I threw water on the flames and woke him from his sleep.",
                "He looked at me with an expression of gratitude and something else, something that I could not quite name, and he thanked me for saving his life.",
                "That night, I realized that I loved him, and though I knew that such a love could never be returned, I could not help the feelings that had taken root in my heart.",
                "Days later, a strange visitor arrived at Thornfield, a beautiful and elegant woman named Blanche Ingram, who had come to stay with Mr. Rochester and his household.",
                "Miss Ingram was clearly intended to be Mr. Rochester's future wife, and she treated me with the casual contempt of a woman who considered herself infinitely above her company.",
                "I watched as she flirted with Mr. Rochester and as he seemed to respond to her attentions, and I felt my heart breaking with each passing hour.",
                "But there was something false in Miss Ingram's charm, and I sensed that her interest in Mr. Rochester was driven more by his fortune than by any genuine affection.",
                "One evening, a stranger arrived at Thornfield, a man named Mr. Mason, who claimed to have business with Mr. Rochester, and from the moment of his arrival, I sensed that something terrible was about to happen.",
                "That night, I was woken by a terrible scream, and I rushed into the corridor to find Mr. Rochester standing there with a candle, his face pale and grim.",
                "He told me that one of the guests had been taken ill, and he asked me to help him tend to the wounded man, and I followed him to a room where Mr. Mason lay bleeding on the bed.",
                "I helped to bind his wounds and to stop the bleeding, and I saw in Mr. Rochester's face a fear that I had never seen before, a fear that seemed to come from the depths of his soul.",
                "As dawn broke, Mr. Rochester helped Mr. Mason to a carriage and sent him away, and I was left with a thousand questions that I did not dare to ask.",
                "Weeks later, Mr. Rochester asked me to walk with him in the garden, and there, beneath the stars, he asked me to marry him.",
                "I could not believe what I was hearing, for I was poor and plain and he was wealthy and powerful, and I told him that I could not possibly be the woman he wanted.",
                "But he insisted that he loved me, that he had loved me from the moment we met, and that he could not imagine his life without me in it.",
                "And so I agreed to marry him, and for a few brief weeks, I was the happiest woman in the world, for I believed that all my dreams had come true.",
                "But there were strange signs that all was not well, and I began to hear whispers in the night, and I began to feel that something terrible was waiting just beyond the edge of my happiness.",
                "On the morning of our wedding, a stranger appeared at the church, and he claimed that Mr. Rochester was already married, and that his wife was still alive.",
                "The truth came out in a rush of horror and shame, and I learned that Mr. Rochester had been hiding his mad wife in the attic of Thornfield, and that she was the source of all the strange sounds I had heard.",
                "I was devastated beyond words, and I knew that I could not marry him, for it would not be right, no matter how much I loved him, no matter how much I wished it could be otherwise.",
                "I left Thornfield that night, with nothing but a few coins in my pocket and no clear idea of where I was going or what I would do when I got there.",
                "And so I began the most difficult journey of my life, a journey that would test every ounce of my strength and every shred of my courage."
            )),
            EnChapter(4, "A New Life", listOf(
                "For three days, I wandered through the countryside, sleeping in fields and begging for food, growing weaker and more desperate with each passing hour.",
                "I had no money and no friends and no family to turn to, and I began to fear that I would die alone in the wilderness, forgotten by the world.",
                "On the fourth day, when I had almost given up hope, I came upon a small house on a hillside, and I knocked on the door and begged for shelter.",
                "The door was opened by a young woman named Diana Rivers, who took one look at my exhausted face and immediately invited me inside.",
                "Her brother, St. John Rivers, and her sister, Mary, welcomed me with a kindness that I had not experienced since Helen's death, and I wept with gratitude.",
                "They gave me food and warm clothes and a bed to sleep in, and they asked me no questions about my past, for they could see that I was in no state to answer them.",
                "For several days, I was too weak to rise from my bed, and the Rivers family nursed me back to health with patience and gentle care.",
                "When I was well enough to speak, I told them my story, though I did not use my real name, for I did not want to be found by Mr. Rochester.",
                "They listened to my tale with sympathy and understanding, and they offered me a place to stay for as long as I needed one.",
                "St. John was a serious and religious man, dedicated to a life of duty and self-sacrifice, and he saw in me a kindred spirit, another soul willing to serve a higher purpose.",
                "He told me about his plans to become a missionary in India, and he asked me whether I would consider joining him in his work.",
                "I did not know how to answer, for I respected his dedication but I did not share his calling, and I did not feel that I could devote my life to a cause that did not stir my heart.",
                "For months, I lived with the Rivers family, and I came to love them as though they were my own kin, and for the first time in my life, I felt as though I belonged somewhere.",
                "St. John found me a position as a teacher at a village school, and though the work was hard and the children were often unruly, I found a quiet satisfaction in my daily labors.",
                "One day, St. John received a letter that had been sent to him by a lawyer in London, and when he read it, his face grew pale and he looked at me with a strange expression.",
                "He told me that our uncle, a man we had never met, had died and left us a fortune of twenty thousand pounds, to be divided equally among us.",
                "I was astonished, for I had never known that I had any relatives, and I had certainly never imagined that I might one day become wealthy.",
                "But even more astonishing was the news that St. John and his sisters were my cousins, that we shared the same blood, that we were family in a way that I had never imagined.",
                "I was overcome with joy, for I had spent my entire life longing for a family, and now I had found one, and I loved them as though I had known them all my life.",
                "I insisted that the fortune be divided equally among the four of us, and though St. John protested at first, he eventually agreed, and we were all made richer by the arrangement.",
                "In the months that followed, St. John grew more and more insistent that I should marry him and accompany him to India, and he argued that such a marriage would be a noble sacrifice.",
                "But I knew that I did not love him, and I knew that he did not love me, and I knew that a marriage without love would be a prison, no matter how noble the cause.",
                "I refused him, and though he was disappointed, he accepted my decision, and we remained friends, and I continued to live with his sisters in the quiet house on the hillside.",
                "One night, as I sat alone in my room, I heard a voice calling my name, a voice that I recognized as Mr. Rochester's, and I felt my heart leap in my chest.",
                "The voice seemed to come from nowhere, and yet it was as clear as if he had been standing beside me, and I knew, in that moment, that I had to find him.",
                "I did not know where he was, or whether he was even alive, but I knew that I could not rest until I had seen him again, and I prepared to leave at once.",
                "I traveled for two days, following the pull of a love that I could not explain and a hope that I could not deny, and on the evening of the second day, I arrived at a place called Ferndean.",
                "It was a small and quiet house, hidden in a forest, and I knew at once that it was where Mr. Rochester was living, though how I knew this, I cannot say.",
                "I knocked on the door, and a servant answered, and when I asked to see Mr. Rochester, she told me that he was in the garden, and she led me to him.",
                "He was sitting on a bench, his eyes bandaged and his face turned toward the setting sun, and I knew at once that he had been blinded in the fire that had destroyed Thornfield.",
                "He did not know that I was there, for he could not see me and he did not hear my footsteps, and I stood for a moment in silence, watching him and weeping.",
                "And then I spoke his name, and he turned toward me, and his face broke into an expression of such joy that I thought my heart would burst.",
                "He asked me whether it was truly me, whether I had come back to him, whether I would stay with him forever, and I told him yes, yes, yes.",
                "We were married a few days later, in a quiet ceremony attended only by a few close friends, and I became Mrs. Rochester, the wife of the man I had loved from the moment I met him.",
                "In the years that followed, his sight slowly returned, and he was able to see the face of our first child, a son, and later a daughter, and he wept with joy at the sight of them.",
                "We lived at Ferndean for many years, in peace and in love, and though we had both suffered much, we had also found in each other the happiness that we had been seeking all our lives.",
                "And so the story of Jane Eyre came to an end, not in sorrow but in joy, not in loss but in fulfillment, not in despair but in the quiet and enduring peace of a love that had survived every trial."
            ))
        )
    )

    // ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
    //  ۲: Wuthering Heights
    // ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
    private fun story2() = EnStory(
        storyId = "adv_wuthering_heights",
        chapters = listOf(
            EnChapter(1, "Mr. Lockwood's Visit", listOf(
                "My name is Mr. Lockwood, and I have recently come to live in the countryside, seeking the quiet and solitude that I believed would restore my health and calm my restless spirit, for I am a man who has always preferred his own company to the company of others.",
                "I have rented a house called Thrushcross Grange, a comfortable and respectable place situated in a valley, and my landlord, I have been told, is a gentleman named Mr. Heathcliff, who lives some four miles away at a place called Wuthering Heights.",
                "One afternoon, I decided to pay a visit to my landlord, for I thought it only proper that I should introduce myself and make his acquaintance, and so I set out on foot across the moors, which were bleak and wild and beautiful in their own strange way.",
                "When I arrived at Wuthering Heights, I found a house that was old and stern, with narrow windows and heavy stone walls, and everything about the place seemed to repel visitors rather than welcome them.",
                "The name 'Wuthering' is a significant provincial adjective, descriptive of the atmospheric tumult to which its station is exposed in stormy weather, and I soon discovered that the house was well named.",
                "I knocked at the door, and after some time, it was opened by a man whose appearance was as forbidding as the house itself, a dark-skinned gypsy in aspect, with black eyes that held an expression of brooding suspicion.",
                "This man was Mr. Heathcliff himself, and when I told him who I was, he did not welcome me with any warmth, but rather regarded me with an expression of cold and guarded hostility.",
                "Nevertheless, he invited me inside, and I found the interior of the house to be as strange and inhospitable as its master, for the rooms were dark and the furniture was heavy and old.",
                "There was a young woman in the house, whom I at first took to be Mr. Heathcliff's wife, but I later learned that she was his daughter-in-law, a widow named Catherine, who seemed to have no love for the man with whom she lived.",
                "As the afternoon wore on, the weather grew worse, and the snow began to fall heavily, and I realized that I would not be able to return to Thrushcross Grange that evening.",
                "Mr. Heathcliff made no offer of hospitality, but the young woman, whose name was also Catherine, took pity on me and told me that I would have to stay the night.",
                "I was given a room that had once belonged to someone named Catherine, and I found it full of strange old books and curious relics, and I began to feel a sense of unease that I could not quite explain.",
                "In the night, I had a terrible dream, or perhaps it was not a dream at all, for it seemed so vivid and so real that I could not tell whether I was asleep or awake.",
                "I dreamed that I heard a voice at the window, a woman's voice, calling out in the darkness, begging to be let in from the cold.",
                "I woke with a start, and I heard the sound again, this time coming from the window itself, and I saw a small white hand reaching through the broken panes.",
                "In a fit of terror, I broke the glass entirely and seized the hand, and I heard a voice crying out, 'Let me in, let me in!'",
                "I shouted for help, and Mr. Heathcliff came running into the room, and when he saw what was happening, his face was transformed by an expression of such anguish that I was struck with pity.",
                "He threw open the window and called out into the storm, begging someone named Catherine to come back, to come home, but there was no answer, only the howling of the wind.",
                "When he turned back to me, his face was once again cold and impassive, and he told me that I had been dreaming, that there was no one at the window, that I had imagined the whole thing.",
                "But I knew what I had seen and heard, and I knew that there was something terrible hidden in the history of this house, something that had left a wound that would not heal.",
                "The next morning, I made my way back to Thrushcross Grange, and I asked my housekeeper, Mrs. Dean, to tell me what she knew about Wuthering Heights and its strange inhabitants.",
                "Mrs. Dean, who had once lived at Wuthering Heights herself, and who had known the family for many years, agreed to tell me the story, and so she began a tale that would haunt me for the rest of my days.",
                "She told me that the story had begun many years before, when the old master of Wuthering Heights, Mr. Earnshaw, had brought home an orphan boy from the streets of Liverpool.",
                "The boy was dark and ragged and spoke a strange language that no one could understand, and he had no name and no family and no past, and Mr. Earnshaw named him Heathcliff.",
                "Heathcliff grew up alongside the two Earnshaw children, Hindley and Catherine, and while Hindley resented him and treated him cruelly, Catherine loved him with a passion that would shape both of their lives forever.",
                "They were wild and free together, running on the moors and dreaming of a life that no one could take from them, and Catherine once said that she loved Heathcliff more than herself, and that he was more herself than she was.",
                "But when Mr. Earnshaw died, Hindley became the master of the house, and he treated Heathcliff as a servant, forcing him to work in the fields and humiliating him at every opportunity.",
                "Catherine, meanwhile, was sent to Thrushcross Grange to recover from an injury, and while she was there, she was introduced to the refined and civilized life of the Linton family.",
                "When she returned to Wuthering Heights, she was changed, no longer the wild girl who had run on the moors with Heathcliff, but a lady with fine clothes and elegant manners.",
                "And though she still loved Heathcliff with all her heart, she began to see him as beneath her, and she began to imagine a life that he could never give her.",
                "When the young Edgar Linton asked her to marry him, she accepted, and Heathcliff, hearing of her decision, ran away from Wuthering Heights in despair.",
                "He did not return for three years, and when he did, he was a different man, hardened by his experiences and consumed by a desire for revenge against those who had wronged him.",
                "He had made a fortune abroad, though no one knew how, and he used his wealth to take control of Wuthering Heights, gambling with the now-drunken Hindley until he had won everything the man possessed.",
                "He also married Edgar Linton's sister, Isabella, though he did not love her, and he treated her with such cruelty that she eventually fled from him.",
                "Catherine, meanwhile, was torn between her love for Heathcliff and her loyalty to her husband, and the conflict drove her into a fever from which she never recovered.",
                "She died shortly after giving birth to a daughter, also named Catherine, and Heathcliff was devastated by her death, and he cursed God and everyone and begged her ghost to haunt him.",
                "He spent the rest of his life pursuing revenge against the families that he believed had wronged him, and he raised his own son, Linton, to be a tool of that revenge.",
                "He forced the younger Catherine to marry Linton, so that he could take control of Thrushcross Grange, and he treated both of them with such cruelty that they were miserable in his presence.",
                "But in the end, his revenge brought him no satisfaction, for he could not find peace without Catherine, and he spent his final days seeing her ghost in every shadow.",
                "He died one night, alone, and his face was found to be twisted into a strange smile, as though he had finally found the peace that he had been seeking all his life.",
                "And so the story of Wuthering Heights came to an end, a story of love and hate and revenge, a story of two families bound together by passion and cruelty.",
                "I thanked Mrs. Dean for her story, and I went to bed that night with a heart full of sorrow, for I knew that I had heard a tale of tragic beauty that would stay with me forever."
            )),
            EnChapter(2, "The Orphan Boy", listOf(
                "Many years before I came to Thrushcross Grange, in the year seventeen seventy-one, the old master of Wuthering Heights, Mr. Earnshaw, made a journey to Liverpool on business.",
                "It was a long and arduous journey, and he was gone for three days, and when he returned, he brought with him a strange and unexpected gift.",
                "He had found a young boy on the streets of Liverpool, a child of six or seven years, ragged and starving and speaking a language that no one could understand.",
                "The boy was dark-skinned and gypsy-like in appearance, and he had no name and no family and no past, and Mr. Earnshaw had taken pity on him and brought him home.",
                "Mrs. Earnshaw was not pleased to see the child, for she had enough trouble with her own two children, and she did not want the burden of another.",
                "Hindley, the older of the two Earnshaw children, was furious, for he saw the boy as a rival for his father's affection, and he hated him from the moment they met.",
                "But Catherine, the younger child, was delighted, for she had always wanted a companion, and she immediately claimed the boy as her own.",
                "Mr. Earnshaw named the boy Heathcliff, though it was not a proper name, but rather a name of the dead son who had died in infancy, and the name stuck.",
                "From the very beginning, there was something wild and untamed about Heathcliff, something that could not be controlled or civilized, something that drew Catherine to him like a moth to a flame.",
                "They became inseparable, running on the moors together, exploring the caves and the cliffs, dreaming of a life that no one could take from them.",
                "Catherine was wild and headstrong, and Heathcliff was silent and brooding, and together they were like two halves of a single soul, bound by a love that neither of them fully understood.",
                "But Mr. Earnshaw grew old and tired, and he began to favor Heathcliff over his own son, and this favoritism made Hindley even more bitter and resentful.",
                "When Mr. Earnshaw died, Hindley became the master of Wuthering Heights, and he immediately set about destroying Heathcliff, whom he had hated since childhood.",
                "He took away Heathcliff's education, and he forced him to work in the fields alongside the servants, and he beat him whenever the mood took him.",
                "Heathcliff bore these cruelties in silence, for he had no choice, but the hatred that he felt for Hindley grew deeper and more bitter with each passing day.",
                "Catherine, meanwhile, remained loyal to Heathcliff, and though she was sometimes ashamed of his lowly position, she could not stop loving him.",
                "And then one night, when they were both young and wild and foolish, they decided to spy on the Linton family at Thrushcross Grange.",
                "They wanted to see how the rich people lived, and they crept through the gardens and peeked through the windows, and they saw a world of luxury and refinement that they had never imagined.",
                "But their adventure ended in disaster, for a dog attacked Catherine, and she was bitten on the ankle, and the Lintons took her into their house to tend her wounds.",
                "Heathcliff was driven away by the servants, and he returned to Wuthering Heights alone, and for five weeks, he did not see Catherine, for she remained at Thrushcross Grange.",
                "When she finally returned, she was not the same girl who had left, for she had been transformed by the Lintons into a young lady with fine clothes and elegant manners.",
                "She had learned to be a lady, and she had also learned to be ashamed of her old life, of the moors and of the wild boy who had once been her closest companion.",
                "And when the young Edgar Linton asked her to marry him, she accepted, for she wanted the wealth and respectability that only a marriage to a gentleman could give her.",
                "But she still loved Heathcliff, and when she confessed this to her nurse, she said that Heathcliff was more herself than she was, and that she could not imagine a life without him.",
                "Heathcliff overheard part of this conversation, but he did not hear the words of love that followed, and he believed that Catherine had betrayed him, and he ran away in despair.",
                "And so the orphan boy of Wuthering Heights disappeared into the night, and no one knew where he had gone, or whether he would ever return.",
                "But he would return, and when he did, he would return as a different man, a man determined to take revenge on all those who had wronged him.",
                "And that revenge would consume him, and it would consume the two families that had treated him so cruelly, and it would end only with his own death.",
                "This was the story that Mrs. Dean told me, and as I listened, I began to understand the dark and terrible secret that lay at the heart of Wuthering Heights.",
                "For the orphan boy had grown into a man of great power and even greater bitterness, and he had returned to wreak havoc on the world that had rejected him.",
                "And I knew, in that moment, that I had stumbled into a story that was much older and much darker than I had ever imagined."
            )),
            EnChapter(3, "A Terrible Choice", listOf(
                "Catherine Earnshaw returned from Thrushcross Grange a changed woman, for the five weeks she had spent in the company of the Linton family had taught her to value things that she had never valued before.",
                "She had learned to dress in fine clothes, to speak in polite phrases, to laugh at genteel jokes, and to present herself as a young lady of refinement and taste.",
                "But in learning these things, she had also learned to be ashamed of her old life, of the wild and untamed existence that she had shared with Heathcliff on the moors.",
                "She still loved Heathcliff, of course, for how could she not? He was her companion, her soulmate, the only person who had ever truly understood her.",
                "But she had also come to love the world that the Lintons represented, a world of wealth and elegance and respectability, a world that Heathcliff could never give her.",
                "And so she was torn between her heart and her head, between love and ambition, between the wild boy of the moors and the gentleman of Thrushcross Grange.",
                "When Edgar Linton asked her to marry him, she said yes, for she knew that marriage to him would give her everything she had ever wanted.",
                "But she also knew that she would never love him as she loved Heathcliff, and she told her nurse, Nelly Dean, that she could not imagine a life without the wild boy who had been her closest friend.",
                "She said that Heathcliff was more herself than she was, that he was the very marrow of her bones, that she could not survive without him.",
                "But Heathcliff did not hear these words of love, for he had already left by the time she spoke them, and he believed that she had betrayed him.",
                "He ran away from Wuthering Heights that night, and no one knew where he had gone, and Catherine was devastated by his disappearance.",
                "She searched for him everywhere, but she could not find him, and she feared that he was dead, that she would never see him again.",
                "She married Edgar Linton, as she had planned, and for a time, she tried to be a good wife, and to forget about the wild boy who had stolen her heart.",
                "But she could not forget him, and she could not be happy, and she spent her days in a state of quiet misery that no one could penetrate.",
                "And then, after three long years, Heathcliff returned, and Catherine was overjoyed to see him, and all her old feelings came rushing back.",
                "But Heathcliff was not the same man who had left, for he had been hardened by his experiences, and he was now consumed by a desire for revenge.",
                "He had made his fortune, though no one knew how, and he was determined to destroy the two families that had wronged him.",
                "He saw Catherine again, and the old passion flared between them, but she was married now, and there could be no going back to what they had once been.",
                "Edgar, understandably, hated Heathcliff, and he forbade Catherine to see him, but Catherine would not obey, for she could not bear to be parted from him again.",
                "And so the two of them continued to meet in secret, and the strain of these meetings began to destroy Catherine's health and her mind.",
                "She became ill, and she began to hallucinate, and she begged Heathcliff to stay with her, to never leave her again, to be with her until the very end.",
                "And Heathcliff stayed, for he loved her more than he loved anything in the world, and he could not bear to see her suffer.",
                "But Catherine died, despite everything, and her death broke Heathcliff in a way that nothing else ever had.",
                "He cursed God and everyone, and he begged Catherine's ghost to haunt him, to stay with him forever, to never leave him alone.",
                "And the ghost did come, for Heathcliff saw her everywhere, in the wind and the rain and the shadows of the house, and he could not find peace.",
                "He spent the rest of his life pursuing revenge against everyone who had wronged him, but none of it brought him any satisfaction.",
                "For in the end, what he wanted most, what he had always wanted most, was Catherine, and she was gone, and no amount of revenge could bring her back.",
                "And so he lived, a haunted man, a man consumed by love and hate, until finally, he too died, and was buried beside her.",
                "And the two of them, Catherine and Heathcliff, are together at last, in death, as they could never be in life."
            )),
            EnChapter(4, "Heathcliff's Revenge", listOf(
                "After Catherine's death, Heathcliff became a man possessed, driven by a hatred so deep and so consuming that it seemed to consume everything it touched.",
                "He had lost the woman he loved, the only person who had ever truly understood him, and in losing her, he had lost the last remaining shreds of his own humanity.",
                "He turned his attention to Hindley, the man who had made his childhood a misery, and he set about destroying him with a cold and calculated cruelty.",
                "He loaned Hindley money, knowing full well that the man would never be able to repay it, and he gambled with him, winning everything that Hindley owned.",
                "Hindley, already broken by the death of his wife and his own weakness for drink, descended into a state of complete ruin, and he died soon after, leaving his son Hareton in Heathcliff's care.",
                "Heathcliff took the boy, not out of any affection, but to complete his revenge, for he intended to raise Hareton as a servant, just as Hindley had raised him.",
                "He treated the boy with a cruelty that was both petty and systematic, denying him education, denying him affection, denying him the dignity that every human being deserves.",
                "And yet, in spite of this cruelty, Hareton grew up to be a good and honest young man, for there was something in his nature that could not be corrupted.",
                "Heathcliff also turned his attention to the Linton family, for he had not forgotten that Edgar Linton had taken Catherine from him.",
                "He married Edgar's sister, Isabella, though he did not love her, and he treated her with a cruelty that drove her to flee from him in terror.",
                "She went to London, and she bore a son, Linton, and when she died, the boy was sent to live with his father, who had no interest in him whatsoever.",
                "Linton was a weak and sickly child, selfish and demanding, and Heathcliff used him as a tool to complete his revenge on the Lintons.",
                "He forced the younger Catherine, the daughter of Edgar and the late Catherine, to marry Linton, so that he could take control of Thrushcross Grange.",
                "Catherine was young and beautiful and full of life, and she did not want to marry the sickly boy, but Heathcliff threatened her, and she had no choice.",
                "She married Linton, and shortly afterward, Linton died, and Heathcliff's plan was complete, for he now controlled both Wuthering Heights and Thrushcross Grange.",
                "But his victory brought him no satisfaction, for the ghosts of his past still haunted him, and he could not find peace.",
                "He saw Catherine everywhere, in the wind and the rain and the shadows of the house, and he began to long for his own death, so that he might join her.",
                "He stopped eating, and he stopped sleeping, and he spent his days walking on the moors, calling out to Catherine, begging her to come to him.",
                "And then, one night, he died, alone in his room, with a strange smile on his face, as though he had finally found the peace that he had been seeking all his life.",
                "He was buried beside Catherine, in the churchyard, and the two of them, who had been separated in life, were finally united in death."
            )),
            EnChapter(5, "Peace at Last", listOf(
                "After Heathcliff died, peace finally returned to the two houses, for the dark and bitter spirit that had haunted them for so many years had at last been laid to rest.",
                "Hareton and the younger Catherine, who had both been treated so cruelly by Heathcliff, found in each other the love and kindness that they had been denied all their lives.",
                "They married on New Year's Day, in the little church between the two houses, and they went to live at Thrushcross Grange, which was now their home.",
                "Hareton, who had never learned to read or write, was taught by his young wife, and he became a kind and gentle man, and he was loved by everyone who knew him.",
                "Catherine, who had lost so much in her short life, found happiness at last in the love of her husband and the peace of her home.",
                "They visited Wuthering Heights from time to time, but they never lived there, for the house held too many dark memories for them to be happy within its walls.",
                "The servants who remained at Wuthering Heights said that they sometimes saw the ghosts of Heathcliff and Catherine walking on the moors, but no one ever knew if this was true.",
                "The old house was kept in good repair, but it was never the same, for the shadows that had once darkened its halls had at last been dispelled.",
                "Mr. Lockwood, who had been told this whole story by Mrs. Dean, returned to London, and he never forgot the tale that he had heard.",
                "He thought often of Catherine and Heathcliff, of the love that had destroyed them both, of the hatred that had consumed everything it touched.",
                "He realized that their love had been real, more real than most people ever experience, and that this was why it had been so powerful and so dangerous.",
                "He understood, at last, that some loves are too great for the world, and that they can only find their fulfillment in death.",
                "The three graves lay side by side in the churchyard, and visitors would often stop and look at them, and wonder about the story that lay behind them.",
                "Edgar Linton, the gentle and patient husband, lay in one grave, and Catherine Earnshaw, who had loved two men and destroyed them both, lay in another.",
                "And Heathcliff, the orphan boy who had become a man of great wealth and greater sorrow, lay in the third grave, beside the woman he had loved all his life.",
                "The wind blew across the moors, as it had always blown, and the heather bloomed purple on the hillsides, as it had always bloomed.",
                "And the story of Wuthering Heights, the story of love and hate and revenge, the story of Catherine and Heathcliff, was told and retold by generations yet to come."
            ))
        )
    )

